# Sn-MOFs/G composite materials for long cycling performance lithium ion batteries

## abstract
摘要: 金属有机骨架 (MOFs) 因其可控制备、比表面积大、孔径可控等优点被认为是最重要的负极材料之一。然而, 由于电子导电率低会导致低的锂存储容量。本文采用沉淀法合成了Sn-MOFs/G。与Sn-MOFs相比, Sn-MOFs/G作为锂离子电池负极具有高的导电性和电化学性能。在1 A·g-1的电流密度下经过500次循环后, 能保持462 mAh·g-1的高比容量, 即使在2A·g-1高电流密度下, 比容量仍能保持265 mAh·g-1。因此, 这种简单有效的合成方法可广泛应用于制备不同的锂离子电池负极材料。

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered as one of the most important anode materials because of their controllable preparation, large specific surface area and controllable aperture. However, low electronic conductivity causes unsatisfied lithium storage capacity. In this paper, the $\mathbf{Sn{-}M O F s/G}$ was synthesized by a simple onestep precipitation. The Sn-MOFs/G samples can enhance electronic conductivity evidently and electrochemical properties as the anode for lithium ion batteries (LIBs) compared to Sn-MOFs. It shows a superior specific capacity of $462\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $1~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles, excellent rate performance of $265\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $2~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ Therefore, the simple and efficient synthesis route could be widely used to prepare different materials for the application as the LIBs anode.
In the past decades, with the development of portable wireless devices, such as mobile phones, laptops and portable instruments, the power sources for portable wireless devices became more and more important in our society. The development of energy storage devices with low cost and eco-friendly is demanded [1, 2]. In all power sources, LIBs are deemed as the best energy storage device due to their high energy density, low price, and long cycle property [3–5]. However, the theoretical capacity for LIBs anode is $372{\mathrm{~mAh}}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ using graphite as the commercial anode [6]. So far, much effort has been devoted to developing new anode materials for LIBs, including various carbon materials [7, 8], alloys [9, 10], metal oxides [11], metal selenides [12], metal sulfides [13], and silicon materials [1, 14, 15]. However, there are still challenges for further improvement of LIBs to satisfy the growing demand for higher performance [16, 17].
MOFs are formed through the organic ligands, and metal ions with high surface area and porosity have potential applications in gas storage, sensors, catalysis, luminescence, energy applications [18–23]. The MOFs have recently attracted great interests as the anode materials of secondary battery, such as LIBs [24], sodium ion batteries [25], Li–S batteries [26], potassium ion batteries [27], and $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries [28]. Up to now, there are two directions for MOFs used in LIBs. One is the precursor being the porous carbon materials [29], metal sulfides [30], metal oxides [31], and metal phosphides [32], and the other is directly being an electrode material [33–35]. For the latter, the metal cations in the MOFs play an active site role in the redox process, and the open crystal frameworks provide efficient and reversible insertion and extraction of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ channel in the electrolytes. Despite their considerable advantages, they still face many challenges in the field of LIBs, such as the small valence change of transition metals and the poor electronic conductivity. Numerous methods had been used to solve the poor electronic conductivity in recent years [36–38]. One of the most popular method is to build nano-hybrids with various carbon materials. Among them, graphene has excellent electronic conductivity and porosity that can buffer the volume expansion to effectively restrain the pulverization and improve the electronic conductivity of active materials [39–41].
In this paper, Sn-based metal–organic frameworks/graphene (Sn-MOFs/G) nanorods were prepared by a simple chemical precipitation method. For a variety of reasons discussed below, we chose stannous sulfate $\mathrm{(SnSO_{4})}$ , 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ , and graphene to prepare Sn-MOFs/G (Fig. 1). (1) As a common active material, Sn can deliver a high theoretical capacity of $992\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ due to the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{22}\mathrm{Sn}_{5}$ alloy with lithium [42]. (2) Graphene is used as the good support materials of Sn-MOFs to avoid the agglomeration due to high specific surface area and outstanding electrical conductivity. Furthermore, they can also buffer the volume expansion during the lithium ion intercalation/deintercalation, which may be advantageous to the cycle stability for LIBs. Therefore, the nanorods as the anode of LIBs showed a high specific capacity of $462\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ after 500 cycles at $1~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and outstanding rate performance of $265\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $2{\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ . The Sn-MOFs/G materials had excellent cycling stability and rate performance as compared to the pure Sn-MOFs.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) are employed to study the morphology and microstructure of Sn-MOFs/G and SnMOFs. SEM images of Sn-MOFs/G and Sn-MOFs (Fig. 2a–c) show that the Sn-MOFs and Sn-MOFs/G have the nanorod-like morphology and the average diameter of about $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . In addition, it should be clear that the surface of Sn-MOFs/G looks rougher than that of Sn-MOFs due to the introduction of graphene. TEM was used to further characterize the structure of the composite of SnMOFs nanorods and graphene nanosheets, as shown in Fig. 2d–f. It should be clear that the Sn-MOFs nanorods scatter on the graphene. This unique structure improves electronic conductivity of Sn-MOFs/G and then releases more specific capacities. Figure $2\mathrm{g-k}$ shows energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mappings, verifying Sn, C, O, and N in the Sn-MOFs/G.
Figure 3a shows X-ray diffractometer (XRD) patterns of pure Sn-MOFs and Sn-MOFs/G. All the diffraction peaks of as-synthesized Sn-MOFs coincide with the reported XRD pattern in other paper [43]. The similar simulated crystal structure has been reported in Ref. [20]. In addition, the diffraction peaks intensity of Sn-MOFs decreases obviously with the introduction of graphene due to the reduction of Sn-MOFs content. We also use Raman scattering to research the structure of pure Sn-MOFs and SnMOFs/G, as shown in Fig. S1a and Fig. 3b. The peaks located at 1604, 1583, 1488, 1442, 1412, 1367, 1160, and $1039~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ can be attributed to the in-plane deformation modes of the $\mathrm{C-H}$ group, the peaks at 861, 839, 810,773, 699, 654, and $582~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be due to the out-of-plane distortion modes of $\mathrm{C-H}$ group, and the peak at $1137~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is attributed to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ tensile distortion mode including the coupling of carboxylate group. However, the relative intensities of these bands are weaker in Sn-MOFs/G than in pure Sn-MOFs. The results show that the structure of SnMOFs has not changed after the introduction of graphene [44]. Besides, the broad D band of graphene displayed at around $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be observed for MOFs/G composites, whereas the G band at around $1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is slightly overlapping with the 1583 and $1604~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ bands of the MOFs. These results suggest the coexistence of graphene component and MOFs in the composites. The Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) method was used to determine the specific surface area value of Sn-MOFs/G using $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ absorption, as shown in Fig. S1b. The adsorption capacity of ${\bf N}_{2}$ has a clear increase in the range of extreme low
Rare Met. (2022) 41(5):1504–1511 relative pressure $(\boldsymbol{p}/p_{0})$ due to the monolayer adsorption in microspores. In the range of other $p/p_{0}$ , the adsorption type is consistent with $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ -type loop line, showing the multilayer adsorption because of the mesoporous and macrospore. The BET surface area of Sn-MOFs and SnMOFs/graphene is about 72 and $68~\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
The enhanced specific surface area of Sn-MOFs/graphene is attributed to the addition of graphene [45]. The chemistry status on the surfaces of Sn-MOFs/G is discussed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Fig. 3). The Sn 3d, O 1s, and C 1s peaks are clearly observed in XPS full spectra of $\mathbf{Sn{-}M O F s/G}$ , as shown in Fig. 3c. The C peak can be fitted to three peaks which are located at 284.6, 285.5, and $288.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the C 1s spectrum, corresponding to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ , C–C, and $_{\mathrm{O-C}}$ , respectively (Fig. 3d). This result illustrates that carbon atom is in Sn-MOFs/G. As shown in Fig. 3e, the high-resolution O 1s spectrum of the Sn$\mathbf{MOFs/G}$ is fitted into three peaks because of the contribution of three types of oxygen. The $532.5~\mathrm{eV}$ peak is due to the hydroxyl groups coming from $\mathrm{Sn(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ lattice; the peak at $531.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ or $\mathrm{\bfC}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{\bfO}$ bonds; and the peak located at $531.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to the characteristic $\mathrm{\bfSn{-}O}$ bond. Figure 3f shows the two peaks at 495.4 and $486.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathrm{Sn}3\mathrm{d}$ that are ascribed to $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ of $\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$ , respectively.
In order to illustrate the lithium ion storage properties of the Sn-MOFs/G electrode, the half-cells are assembled using CR2032 coin cells at room temperature. Figure 4a displays the cyclic voltammetry (CV) profiles of Sn-MOFs/ G nanorods obtained in the first 5 cycles at a scanning speed of $0.1\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ with the potential range of $0.1{-}3.0\ \mathrm{V}$ . A reduction peak is observed at about $0.11~\mathrm{V}$ in the first cycle that corresponds to the solid–electrolyte–interface film (SEI) formation. There are two well-defined oxidation peaks located at about 0.58 and $1.36\mathrm{~V~}$ that can be due to the alloying/dealloying process of Sn and conversion reaction of $\mathrm{{{Sn/Sn}^{4+}}}$ , respectively. It is obvious that the CV curves are almost overlapping after the first cycle, indicating that the Sn-MOFs/G as anode of LIBs has very steady electrochemical performance.
Figure $4\mathsf{b}$ displays the 1st, 2nd, 5th, 50th, 100th, and 200th galvanostatic discharge/charge curves for the Sn$\mathbf{MOFs/G}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The first discharge specific capacity is $983\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the charge specific capacity is $252\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with the low columbic efficiency (CE) of $25.6\%$ . We can observe the low initial CE $(25.6\%)$ of Sn$\mathbf{MOFs/G}$ due to the formation of the SEI film and the irreversible reaction of the electrode material during the first charge and discharge, which is also common in other electrode materials. The first capacity loss of Sn-MOFs/G should be attributed to the SEI film formation due to the inevitable electrolyte decomposition. There are two plateaus at about 0.5 and $0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ in the initial discharge curve, respectively, which is in accordance with the initial CV curve. From Fig. 4b, we can see that the specific capacity is increasing from 298 to $703\ \mathrm{mAh}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with the increase of cycle number from 2 to 200. This result illustrates that the active materials are gradually activated [46].
The rate capability of Sn-MOFs and Sn-MOFs/G is tested in different current density, as seen in Fig. 4c. It can be delivered that the specific capacities are 615, 567, 480, 393, $265\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at current densities of 100, 200, 500, 1000, $2000\ \mathrm{mA{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , respectively, higher than those of SnMOFs (585, 406, 209, 96, and $28\ \mathrm{mAh\cdotg^{-1}}$ at different current densities). Furthermore, when the current density returns to the original value of $100\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , the discharge specific capacity can be back to about $784~\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ indicating good reversibility of Sn-MOFs/G. Interestingly, after the first cycle, high CE $(100\%)$ is obtained. The long cycle performance of Sn-MOFs/G over 500 cycles at $1~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is also investigated. As a contrast, the pure SnMOFs is only left a low specific capacity of $10\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ over 500 cycles (Fig. 4d). The Sn-MOFs/G electrode displays high reversible discharge capacity of $462\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ over 500 cycles and the rate of capacity retention is higher than $87.2\%$ , accompanying with capacity decay rate of only $0.0257\%$ each cycle. Moreover, the columbic efficiency is close to $100\%$ , suggesting the outstanding cycle capability. Compared with other Sn-based materials, SnMOFs/G as the anode of LIBs has excellent electrochemical performance, as shown in Table S1.
In order to study the reaction kinetics of Sn-MOFs and Sn-MOFs/G, electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) are measured in the range of $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}\mathrm{-}100~\mathrm{kHz}$ . Figure S2 shows the Nyquist plots of Sn-MOFs and Sn-MOFs/G. The equivalent circuit model is displayed in the inset of Fig. S2. At high-frequency area, there is a broad semicircle that can be ascribed to the result of the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , the resistance relevant to the formation of the solid electrolyte interfacial $(R_{\mathrm{e}})$ , and the double-layer capacitor (CPE). And a straight line is found at low-frequency area due to the Warburg impedance $(Z_{\mathrm{w}})$ . The fitting $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of Sn-MOFs/G $(113.2\Omega)$ is significantly less than that of SnMOFs $(263.1\ \Omega)$ . It is obvious that the electrode shows lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ because of the introduction of the graphene in the Nyquist plots of fresh cells. Based on the above results, the introduction of graphene can improve the electrical conductivity and is well suited for the insertion/extraction reaction in the process of LIBs materials.
In order to further explain the effect of morphology on the electrode electrochemical performance, we disassembled the batteries and used SEM to characterize the morphology of Sn-MOFs/G electrodes after 1 and 10 cycles, as shown in Fig. S3. It could be obviously noticed that SnMOFs/G composite kept the original nanowires structure after 10 cycles. It is obvious that Sn-MOFs/G as the anode material exhibits better rate properties and cycle stability than the pure Sn-MOFs due to the introduction of graphene. Hence, the excellent electrochemical properties of the Sn-MOFs/G can be attributed to the synergistic effect between Sn-MOFs and graphene. The Sn-MOFs contribute to high battery capacity, and the graphene stabilizes the long cycle stability of the Sn-MOFs/G. Besides the graphene can provide sufficient electrochemically active sites for the storage of lithium ions, accelerate the transmission of electrons and lithium ions, and buffer the volume expansion of electrode materials [47–49], it can prevent the pulverization of the electrode materials during charging and discharging process. At last, the graphene also offers shorter diffusion pathway for Li ions in SnMOFs/G.
To further analyze the results of the outstanding rate properties of Sn-MOFs/G, CV curves under different sweep rates were measured to investigate the electrochemical kinetics for LIBs. CV curves of Sn-MOFs/G electrode at different sweep rates (0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, $1\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}})$ , as illustrated in Fig. 5a, obviously show that there is a similar shape for all CV curves at different sweep rates. To explain this phenomenon, the relationship between current $(i)$ and sweep rate (v) can be calculated by the following equations:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{i=a\nu^{b}}}\\ {{\lg i=b\lg\nu+\lg a}}\end{array}
$$
where $a$ is adjustable constant, and $b$ reflects the slope of fitted lgi vs. lgv curves. Through literature reports, the $b$ value close to 0.5 showed that the process is mainly controlled by diffusion, while close to 1 indicated the capacitive controlled process [50–52]. The value of $b$ can be calculated through Eq. (2), as shown in Fig. 5b. The values of $b$ are 0.82 and 0.70 for oxidation peak and reduction peak, respectively, showing that the process is mainly capacitive controlled in the Sn-MOFs/G anode. The concrete percentage of capacitive contribution can be calculated by the following equations:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{i(V)=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}}}\\ {{i(V)/\nu^{1/2}=k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}}}\end{array}
$$
where $k_{1}\nu$ and $k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ correspond to capacitance and diffusion contribution, respectively. As shown in Fig. 5c, the CV curve of Sn-MOFs/G electrode can be separated to two parts, including the surface-induced capacitance and ion diffusion.
The percentage of capacitance is about $70.4\%$ with the sweep rate of $0.8~\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 5d, the capacitive contribution increases from $33.6\%$ to $79.2\%$ with the sweep rate increasing from 0.1 to $1.0\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The high capacitive controlled results are attributed to the short ion diffusion length and fast charge transfer speed due to the introduction of high conductive graphene and high surface area in Sn-MOFs/G.
In summary, a feasible precipitation method is used to synthesize the Sn-MOFs/G electrode material for LIBs. The Sn-MOFs/G shows excellent electrochemical properties including prominent battery capacity, notable rate performance, and distinct cyclic stability due to the introduction of graphene. It can enhance the electric conductivity and successfully relieve the stress of volume expansion. It can deliver a discharge specific capacity of $703\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ over 200 cycles at $100\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and exhibits a reversible discharge capacity as high as $462\ \mathrm{mAh}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $1000\ \mathrm{mA{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ over 1000 cycles. This demonstrated that the Sn-MOFs/G has enormous potential applications as a highperformance anode material for LIBs.
